Create exactly these 10 components:

091 Button Group
092 Icon Button Group
093 Split Button
094 Segmented Control
095 Action Group
096 Action Bar
097 Floating Action
098 Overflow Action
099 Command Action
100 Destructive Action Group

These are action-composition primitives.

They must compose existing controls rather than recreate their internal button behavior.

Do not create:
- complete toolbars
- complete navigation bars
- complete command palettes
- complete contextual menus
- application-specific toolbars
- dashboard action areas
- page headers
- complete mobile bottom navigation

Those belong to higher-level groups.

The components must work across:

Mobile
Tablet
Desktop
Large Desktop
Ultra Wide

Support:
Mouse
Keyboard
Touch
Stylus
Screen readers

Support:
Light
Dark
High Contrast
RTL
Localization
Dynamic text
Browser zoom
Reduced motion
Reduced transparency

Every interactive control must provide:
default
hover where applicable
focus
pressed
disabled
selected where applicable

Do not rely only on hover because touch devices do not have hover.

Every touch-accessible action must respect the global minimum effective touch target.

Do not create tiny controls simply to fit more actions.

Action hierarchy must be obvious.

Primary actions must visually dominate secondary actions.

Secondary actions must not visually compete with destructive or primary actions.

Avoid:
unnecessary gradients
heavy shadows
excessive borders
large decorative surfaces
excessive pill styling
random icon usage

Every icon must communicate a recognizable action or state.

Do not add icons merely for decoration.

Icons must have accessible names when they communicate meaning.

Icon-only controls must have accessible labels.

Do not use an icon-only control when a text label is necessary for discoverability.

Actions must have predictable ordering.

On narrow screens:
actions may wrap
actions may collapse
secondary actions may move into overflow

Do not allow primary actions to disappear into overflow unless explicitly configured.

Support localization.

Text can become significantly longer in other languages.

Do not design around fixed button widths.

Support RTL using logical layout properties.

Do not manually reverse icon placement when the icon itself is directional unless the semantic meaning requires mirroring.

Actions that open menus must clearly communicate that a menu exists.

Actions that trigger immediate operations must not visually resemble menu triggers.

Keyboard interaction must follow established platform conventions.

Do not invent unusual keyboard behavior.

Focus must remain visible after keyboard navigation.

Destructive actions require stronger confirmation when the operation is irreversible.

Do not turn every destructive action into a confirmation dialog automatically; the consuming component decides the appropriate confirmation pattern.

The components must remain generic enough to compose into future:
toolbars
editors
tables
dashboards
detail pages
settings
AI interfaces
mobile actions
contextual actions